        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: cohors, cohortis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cohors, cohortis — \"a cohort; an enclosure\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Cohors ad portam castrōrum stābat. A cohort was standing at the gate of the camp."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, 3rd declension. A legion had ten cohorts. The same word meant a farmyard, which gives English court."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cohors is inherited, and its earlier history is not settled. It first meant \"an enclosure, a farmyard\" (EtymDB, Lewis & Short), and then the body of soldiers drawn up together. English court comes from it through French, and cohort directly."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
